e kernel deadlock rather than a prolonged lock contention or another kernel/network subsystem failure.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Shutdown or reboot became stuck because of the same kernel condition.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"The node rebooted successfully but its management transport failed to recover.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["On the fresh node, immediately capture OS, kernel, boot ID, lsblk, free space, and existing crypttab/fstab state; keep the installed release and kernel unchanged.","Before starting package work, use short timeouts to test an RTNL query and DNF responsiveness. Avoid launching concurrent DNF health checks and installation commands. If the same stall is already present, stop early and report it rather than accumulating blocked processes.","Install the distribution-provided cryptsetup package while the fresh node is responsive. Confirm the executable and package version before modifying storage.","Because the specified layout has no spare disk, use a sensibly sized file-backed LUKS container on the root filesystem, create an XFS filesystem inside its mapper device, and mount it at /srv/secure. Check free space before allocating it.","Create a root-only key file under /etc/cryptsetup-keys.d, reference the backing file and key in /etc/crypttab, and reference the mapper device in /etc/fstab. A local key enables unattended boot but provides weaker protection if an attacker obtains the root disk and key together.","Before rebooting, validate crypttab/fstab syntax, reload systemd, activate and mount the volume, and verify findmnt, cryptsetup status, permissions, and a write/read test.","Perform one native reboot only after the live configuration works. Prove success with a changed boot ID plus findmnt /srv/secure, cryptsetup status, filesystem type, and a post-boot write/read test. Do not claim reboot persistence from connection loss alone."]}
